{
  "description": "Threat model for Dustnet. Authoritative source for the adversaries assumed hostile and for the control and evidence answering each. Read by tools/allocation-audit: an adversary with no coverage row, a row naming an undeclared property, or a row whose evidence is neither a test function nor a make target fails the build. docs/spec/07-security.md describes the controls in prose. Adversaries are grouped by which side faces them: the first group is the client, which trusts nothing a server sends; the second is a server built on dustnet-server that has installed hooks, which trusts nothing a visitor sends. dustnetd installs no hooks, so the second group's adversaries cannot reach it at all -- the without_a_* tests hold it to that -- but they are listed here because the library's guarantees to a hooked server are part of the supported boundary and would otherwise be claimed only in prose.",
  "trusted": [
    "operating system",
    "terminal emulator"
  ],
  "out_of_scope": [
    "compromised hosts",
    "malicious operator-installed server extensions"
  ],
  "properties": [
    "origin isolation",
    "terminal integrity",
    "bounded resource use",
    "local confidentiality",
    "content integrity",
    "credential containment"
  ],
